mode: "100644"
text: "# Examples — teardowns in practice\n\nWorked teardowns showing the framework, the counterfactual, the replicability check, and the\napplied principle — including a non-replicable case. Voice context is the blunt indie-founder voice.\n\n---\n\n## Example 1 — A replicable teardown (\"I quit my job\" founder video, 4M views)\n\n**Layers:**\n- Hook (0–2s): \"I quit a $200k job to build something nobody asked for.\" — bold, high-stakes.\n- Emotional/share driver: **identity + inspiration** (viewers project their own escape fantasy).\n- Structure: open loop (\"here's what happened\") → honest arc → payoff.\n- Format: talking head, handheld, native.\n- Topic/angle: relatable founder leap.\n- Share-trigger: **identity** — people share it to signal their own ambitions.\n- Distribution: mid-size account, no trending sound, normal timing.\n\n**Counterfactual:** remove the sound → still works (no sound). Flatten the hook → dies. Strip the\nidentity/inspiration angle → just another vlog. **Drivers = the load-bearing hook + identity/\ninspiration trigger.** Format and topic supporting; nothing incidental drove it.\n\n**Replicability:** mid-size account, no one-time event, the mechanism recurs across many founder\nvideos → **transferable.**\n\n**Principle → applied:** *a high-stakes, identity-driven hook + an inspiration trigger.* Your niche\nversion: \"We deleted our most-requested feature to keep the product simple\" — same mechanism (bold\nstakes + an identity/values trigger for builders), your subject. → `tiktok-script` / `hook-writer`.\n\n---\n\n## Example 2 — Isolating the real driver (the \"it was the sound\" trap)\n\n**Ask:** \"It went viral because of the trending sound — let's just use the sound.\"\n**Teardown:** the sound was on thousands of flopped videos too. Counterfactual: a flat video with\nthe *same* sound wouldn't have popped. The driver was a **relatable hook** (\"POV: the feature\neveryone begged for, that nobody used\") + **recognition** trigger. The sound was incidental.\n**Lesson:** copy the *hook + relatability mechanism*, not the sound. Copying the sound reproduces the\nnoise, not the success.\n\n---\n\n## Example 3 — Non-replicable (flag honestly)\n\n**Ask:** \"An 8M-follower creator's post blew up — what's the formula?\"\n**Response:** Most of this is **account size + reach they already had** — a 200-follower account doing\nthe identical post likely gets a fraction of it. There may be a small structural lesson (a clean\nhook), but the \"formula\" here is largely the audience, plus possible timing luck. I won't hand you a\nfalse principle. To find what's actually transferable, let's tear down several pieces that hit from\n*small* accounts in your niche and look for the mechanism that recurs.\n\n---\n\n## Example 4 — Your own overperformer (small-sample caution)\n\n**Ask:** \"Our post got 5× our usual views — what do we repeat?\"\n**Teardown (from visible signals):** the standout was a contrarian hook + a practical-value\nshare-trigger (people sent it to colleagues). **But** one post is an anecdote — repeat the\n*mechanism* (contrarian + useful) across a few more and see if it holds before declaring it your\nformula. (No WoopSocial analytics — judged from native insights you pulled.)\n\n---\n\n## What these share\n\n- The **counterfactual** isolates 1–2 real drivers from the noise.\n- The **replicability check** catches account-size/luck/one-time confounds and small samples.\n- The output is a **transferable mechanism applied to the user's niche** — never a copy.\n- **Honest scope**: visible signals, no analytics claims, no virality guarantees.\n"
